POSTGRES_USER=fudrouter
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=fudrouter
POSTGRES_DB=fudrouter

# Valkey — shared host-native instance (systemd valkey-server.service,
# 127.0.0.1:6379, requirepass). fudrouter uses logical DB 1 so its keyspace
# stays separate from other projects on the same server. Never hardcoded in
# app code (docs/specs/2026-09-30-platform-schema.md §12).
VALKEY_URL=redis://:valkey_dev@127.0.0.1:6379/1

# Provider credentials master key (openssl rand -base64 32). The data plane
# cannot start without it: every provider blob in the credentials_enc column is
# AES-256-GCM under this key. MUST be identical across the router, the workers
# and the scheduler, or none of them can read the others' blobs.
CREDENTIALS_MASTER_KEY=

# Rotation window only — leave unset in the steady state. Set it to the OLD key
# alongside the NEW CREDENTIALS_MASTER_KEY, restart, re-encrypt every stored
# blob, then remove it. While set, decryption tries active first, then this one,
# so old rows keep working while all writes already use the new key. A malformed
# value fails startup loudly rather than silently orphaning old rows
# (services/router/internal/crypt/crypt.go, NewWithPrev).
#CREDENTIALS_MASTER_KEY_PREV=

# Router ingress trust (services/router/internal/proxy/lifecycle.go,
# TrustedProxiesEnv). The addresses whose X-Forwarded-For entries the data plane
# may believe, as a comma-separated list of IPs and CIDRs — the operator's
# declaration of "my TLS terminates here". Unset means trust nothing: XFF is
# ignored entirely and request_log.client_ip records RemoteAddr, which FAILS
# CLOSED (a client forging XFF cannot defeat a key's ip_allowlist or poison the
# logs). That is correct for a direct deployment, but WRONG behind the
# documented cloudflared tunnel — every logged IP would be the tunnel's. A
# proxied deployment sets this to the proxy's address, e.g. 127.0.0.1 for a
# local cloudflared/tunnel, or the ingress CIDR.
#ROUTER_TRUSTED_PROXIES=